---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
---
<Base
  title="MetaFlux — Derivatives, on first principles."
  description="An independent L1 derivatives exchange. Perpetuals and spot from one account with one margin number, finality in a single block, a parallel EVM in every block."
  canonical="https://mtf.exchange/"
  script={false}
>
<Fragment slot="head">
<script src="/hero.js" is:inline defer></script>
</Fragment>
<Nav />

<header class="hero">
  <div>
    <div class="status rise">Independent L1 · testnet live</div>
    <h1 class="rise">Derivatives, on<br><em>first principles</em>.</h1>
  </div>
  <div class="side rise d1">
    <p>An independent L1 whose state machine is a derivatives exchange. Perpetuals and spot from one account with one margin number, and finality in a single block.</p>
    <div class="row">
      <a class="btn" href="https://app.mtf.exchange/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Trade →</a>
      <a class="more" href="/whitepaper.html">Whitepaper</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="field rise d2" aria-hidden="true">
  <div class="fallback"></div>
  <canvas></canvas>
  <div class="legend"><i></i><b>Block committed</b> · final, no confirmations</div>
</div>

<div class="wrap">
  <div class="stats">
    <div><b>0</b><span>confirmations to finality</span></div>
    <div><b>2<small>/</small>3</b><span>of stake behind every block</span></div>
    <div><b>1 tx</b><span>to list a market</span></div>
    <div><b>1</b><span>margin number per account</span></div>
  </div>

  <section class="sec reveal" id="desk">
    <div class="kicker">The desk</div>
    <h2>Trade perpetuals and spot from a single account, against one margin number.</h2>
    <p class="lead">A SPAN-style scenario engine values your whole book at once, so a hedge stops paying margin twice. The book, the ticket, the blotter and the account summary run live off the testnet today.</p>
    <div class="cta"><a class="btn" href="https://app.mtf.exchange/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Trade →</a></div>
    <div class="shot-wrap"><div class="shot"><img src="/shots/desk.webp" alt="The MetaFlux testnet trading desk: a BTC-USDC perpetual with its candle chart, live order book, order ticket, a four-position blotter and the account summary." loading="lazy"></div></div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec reveal" id="why">
    <div class="kicker">Why a chain, not a contract</div>
    <h2>Matching, margin, liquidation and settlement run inside consensus.</h2>
    <div class="pillars">
      <div>
        <div class="kicker">Risk</div>
        <h3>One number, not three.</h3>
        <p>Cross-asset portfolio margin values the book as a whole. Same three positions, same equity — only the maintenance margin moves.</p>
        <a class="more" href="/whitepaper.html#risk">How the engine values a book →</a>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="kicker">Liquidation</div>
        <h3>A warning before a liquidation.</h3>
        <p>The engine reports a band, not a verdict. The first tier cancels resting orders and touches no position — a block to react, not a tick.</p>
        <a class="more" href="/whitepaper.html#risk">The four tiers →</a>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="kicker">Execution</div>
        <h3>The EVM, made parallel.</h3>
        <p>Your contract call never queues behind a market it doesn't touch. Portfolio margin, ADL pricing and vault NAV are native precompiles, not 80,000-gas Solidity.</p>
        <a class="more" href="/whitepaper.html#vm">The execution layer →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>

<section class="vision">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="reveal">MetaFlux starts from one premise: a derivatives exchange should be a chain, <em>not a contract on one</em>.</p>
    <p class="reveal">When the clearing engine is the state machine, the rules of the market are the rules of the ledger — deterministic, auditable, final in one block.</p>
    <p class="reveal">The EVM shares every block, so strategies, vaults and market listings are programs, not permissions. Anyone can list a market; the winning bid is burned, and nobody is paid to approve you.</p>
    <p class="reveal">Components ship across phased testnet milestones. Where this site describes something not yet live, the whitepaper says when.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="wrap">
  <section class="sec reveal" id="security">
    <div class="kicker">Security</div>
    <h2>MetaFlux is secured by MetaFluxBFT, a stake-weighted BFT protocol with immediate finality.</h2>
    <p class="lead">A quorum certificate needs two-thirds of bonded stake, so safety holds unconditionally against any adversary below one-third. Leadership rotates every round; a faulty leader costs one round, never the chain. Double-signing is self-evidencing from two conflicting signed votes and slashes 5% of stake. Floating point, unordered iteration and wall-clock reads are prohibited in the consensus path.</p>
    <figure class="arch"><img src="/arch.svg" alt="MetaFlux architecture: clients, access layer, clearing core, execution, MetaFluxBFT consensus" loading="lazy"></figure>
  </section>

  <section class="sec reveal" id="build">
    <div class="kicker">Builders</div>
    <div class="integrate">
      <div>
        <h2>Integrate MetaFlux.</h2>
        <p class="lead">One gateway serves <span class="mono">POST /info</span>, <span class="mono">POST /exchange</span>, WebSocket and EVM JSON-RPC. Orders are signed in a standard EIP-712 envelope, so hardware signers and browser wallets work unmodified. Agent wallets sign every order and never hold withdrawal authority. Deploying a market is one transaction under MIP-3.</p>
        <div class="links">
          <a class="more" href="https://docs.mtf.exchange/">Docs →</a>
          <a class="more" href="https://docs.mtf.exchange/integration/migrating-from-hl">Migration guide →</a>
          <a class="more" href="https://docs.mtf.exchange/mip/mip-3">MIP-3 →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
<pre class="code"><code><span class="k">import</span> &#123; MetaFluxClient &#125; <span class="k">from</span> <span class="s">"@metaflux/sdk"</span>;
<span class="c">// one gateway, one chain id — testnet is open today</span>
<span class="k">const</span> client = <span class="k">new</span> MetaFluxClient(&#123;
  gateway: <span class="s">"https://api.devnet.mtf.exchange"</span>,
  chainId: 114514,
  signer, <span class="c">// agent wallet — never withdrawal authority</span>
&#125;);
<span class="k">await</span> client.exchange.submitOrder(order); <span class="c">// same strategy, new wire</span></code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec reveal" id="faq">
    <div class="kicker">FAQ</div>
    <div class="faq">
      <details>
        <summary>What is MetaFlux, exactly?</summary>
        <p>An independent L1 derivatives chain. Its own validator set, its own BFT consensus, its own EVM execution layer. Built from first principles for derivatives clearing, not borrowed from a general-purpose L1.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Which parts of this are shipping today?</summary>
        <p>Components ship across phased testnet milestones — the testnet runs the clearing core, the parallel EVM and the book; the rest lands on the sequence the whitepaper sets out. Where this site describes something that isn't live yet, the whitepaper says when.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>When does mainnet launch?</summary>
        <p>Mainnet follows audit and testnet milestones, not calendar dates. The testnet is live and open for integration now; six months of public testnet precede the TGE, with phased validator onboarding and the bridge audit as the explicit gating item.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What does integration look like?</summary>
        <p>MetaFlux speaks its own MTF-native protocol — one gateway serving POST /info, POST /exchange, WebSocket, and EVM JSON-RPC. The SDKs (TypeScript and Rust, preview) absorb the wire rewrite; agent wallets, sub-accounts, vaults, cross and isolated margin and client order ids map one to one.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Who is the testnet for?</summary>
        <p>Anyone — there is no application and no allow-list. Take test USDC from the faucet, point your client at the gateway, and trade. We especially want to hear from market makers and teams shipping production trading systems: <a href="mailto:builders@mtf.exchange">builders@mtf.exchange</a>.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>

